<TopicPage slug="subjects/information-technology/foundational/types-and-components-of-computer-systems">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every computer system — from a desktop under a desk to the smartphone in a pocket — is one
    idea repeated: <strong>hardware</strong>, the physical components, running
    <strong>software</strong>, the programs that control the operation of the computer and
    process the data. Cambridge IGCSE ICT 0417 opens with exactly this distinction (section 1.1)
    and then dissects the machine: the CPU that processes instructions (1.2), the operating
    system that lets you talk to it (1.3), the types of computer those parts get packed into
    (1.4), and the emerging technologies — artificial intelligence and extended reality — that
    are redrawing the category (1.5).
  </p>
  <p>
    The deep idea to carry through the whole chapter is a division of labour. Hardware can only
    do a short list of primitive things — move a number from one place to another, add or
    compare two numbers, jump to a different instruction — at immense speed. Software is the
    arrangement of those primitives into anything you recognise as "using a computer": writing
    an essay, editing a photo, playing a game. Nothing else in computing is mysterious once you
    hold that pairing: the processor is the worker, memory is the workbench, storage is the
    filing cabinet, the operating system is the supervisor who decides which job gets the
    worker next, and the applications are the services delivered on top. This chapter walks the
    stack from the bottom up.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> hardware and software are not interchangeable labels for
  "physical versus digital things you can see". A printer's driver (software that lets the OS
  operate the printer hardware) is software even though it ships on a disc; a "hard copy" is
  hardware output. The test is functional: does the component <em>do</em> the work (hardware) or
  <em>direct and enable</em> it (software)?</p>

  <h2>Inside the machine: fetch, decode, execute</h2>
  <p>
    The CPU's job, in the syllabus's words, is processing instructions entered into the computer
    in order to produce an output. It does this with a three-beat loop — fetch, decode, execute —
    shared between its two headline parts: the control unit that sequences and directs, and the
    arithmetic and logic unit that computes. The loop's memory partners matter as much as the
    loop itself, and the RAM/ROM split is a guaranteed exam question. Walk the whole cycle.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the fetch–decode–execute cycle, plus the two memories that make it possible — RAM and ROM" widget="equationStep" config={fdeCfg} />

  <p>
    The memory split is worth stating as a contrast table you can recite: <strong>RAM</strong> —
    read/write, volatile, holds what is currently in use, contents lost on power-off;
    <strong>ROM</strong> — read-only, non-volatile, holds the bootstrap, contents fixed at
    manufacture. One is a desk you work on; the other is the instructions glued to the inside of
    the machine's lid telling it how to wake up. Everything else the syllabus lists as hardware
    — motherboard, graphics card, sound card, network interface card, storage devices, input and
    output devices — exists to feed the cycle or carry its results. The motherboard is the
    physical joining: buses (address, data, control) running between CPU, memory and the slots
    where expansion cards plug in. The storage hierarchy is worth learning as a speed ladder:
    CPU registers (fastest, smallest) → RAM (fast, volatile) → SSD (fast-ish, permanent) →
    hard disk (slower, cheap per gigabyte, permanent). Each step down buys capacity with
    latency — which is why opening an app "loads it into RAM": dragging the program up the
    ladder so the CPU can fetch from it at full speed.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> RAM is not the same as storage, and "memory" is not the same
  as "space". A machine with 8 GB of RAM and a 256 GB SSD has 8 GB of <em>workbench</em> and
  256 GB of <em>filing cabinet</em>. Confusing them leads to the classic wrong diagnosis:
  a full disk is not "out of memory" — though it can still crash a system that uses disk space
  as overflow when RAM fills.</p>

  <h2>The software half, and the operating system that runs it</h2>
  <p>
    Software divides into two families. <strong>Applications software</strong> provides the
    services the <em>user</em> requires to solve a task — word processing, spreadsheets,
    databases, video and graphics editing, CAD, web browsers. <strong>System software</strong>
    provides the services the <em>computer</em> requires to operate — operating systems, device
    drivers, utilities, compilers and linkers. The test that separates them: could this program
    exist without a user? A spreadsheet exists for the user; a driver that translates "print
    page 2 in duplex" into signals a specific printer model understands exists for the machine.
    The families also interact in a fixed order: the OS starts first (from ROM's bootstrap),
    loads drivers for the hardware present, and only then can applications launch and request
    services — memory allocation, file access, screen drawing, networking — through the OS
    rather than touching hardware directly. That indirection is why the same application runs
    on many different machines: it talks to the OS's stable interface, not to the metal.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>operating system</strong> itself is the bridge, and its core jobs can be
    listed: manage memory, manage files and directories, schedule which program gets the CPU,
    handle input and output, manage security and user accounts, and provide the user
    interface. Through a <strong>command line interface</strong> (CLI) you type the
    instructions — terse, scriptable, powerful, and still how servers are administered;
    through a <strong>graphical user interface</strong> (GUI) you point and click — discoverable,
    visual, hungry for screen and processing resources. Dialogue-based and gesture-based
    interfaces (talk to it, swipe it) now cover everything from smart speakers to tablets. Same
    machine, different contract with the human — and each interface is a genuine trade-off:
    the GUI trades expert speed for learnability, the CLI the reverse.
  </p>

  <h2>Types of computer: one machine, many packages</h2>
  <p>
    The types of computer in syllabus 1.4 — desktop, laptop, tablet, smartphone, phablet — are
    not really different machines; they are the same machine at different points of a
    decades-long shrink, and the syllabus wants you to evaluate them against each other. The
    <strong>desktop</strong> trades portability for everything else: cheap expandability
    (standard slots and connectors), the best cooling and hence the best sustained performance,
    easy repair. The <strong>laptop</strong> folds the same architecture into a battery-powered
    clamshell and pays for it in price, repair difficulty and thermal limits. The
    <strong>tablet</strong> and <strong>smartphone</strong> push further into
    <em>portability</em> — instant-on, always-connected, sensor-rich (GPS, accelerometer,
    camera) — and pay in screen size, input precision and upgradeability (usually none). A
    <strong>phablet</strong> blurs the tablet/phone line with a large phone-size screen. The
    professional skill the section tests is matching type to task: a film editor needs the
    desktop's sustained power; a surveyor in the field needs the phone's portability and GPS;
    a student writing essays sits between them with the laptop. Note also the boundary cases
    the syllabus mentions in passing: mainframe-style servers (the same fetch–decode–execute
    cycle, scaled up in racks) and embedded computers (a washing machine's controller is the
    same idea frozen into one fixed program in ROM).
  </p>

  <h2>Why your phone exists: one line on a log plot</h2>
  <p>
    The shrink that produced the smartphone has a quantitative engine, and the plot below is
    it: the number of transistors on a flagship CPU on a log<sub>10</sub> scale — each gridline
    is ten times more transistors. Intel's 4004 of 1971 carried about 2,300 (log<sub>10</sub> ≈
    3.36); the 2022 Apple M1 Ultra carries about 114 billion (log<sub>10</sub> ≈ 11.06). A
    roughly straight line on a log scale is exponential growth — <strong>Moore's law</strong>,
    Gordon Moore's 1965 observation that the component count on integrated circuits was
    doubling roughly every two years — and it is the reason a 1970s computer filled a room
    that today fits in a phone, trading the desktop's expandability for the mobile computer's
    portability.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="transistor counts of flagship CPUs, 1971–2022, on a log10 scale — a straight line means exponential growth" widget="plotter" config={mooreCfg} />

  <p>
    Exponential growth is deceptive in exactly this plotting, so read the graph with care: each
    equal vertical step is a tenfold jump, so the distance from the 1993 Pentium to the 2000
    Pentium 4 — seven years on the horizontal axis — represents the transistor count
    multiplying roughly thirteen times. The same
    curve explains price: while a fab (fabrication plant) grows more expensive every generation,
    the cost <em>per transistor</em> kept falling for decades — which is how computation became
    cheap enough to embed in doorbells and trainers.
  </p>

  <h2>Emerging technologies: what the curve buys</h2>
  <p>
    Section 1.5 asks about the impact of emerging technologies, and two headline it. First,
    <strong>artificial intelligence</strong> — machine learning systems trained on examples
    rather than programmed with rules. The transistor budget is what buys it: modern AI runs on
    processors doing arithmetic in parallel at staggering scale, which is why voice assistants,
    translation, image recognition and large language models all arrived on the same decade the
    graph's last points cover. Its impact questions are the syllabus's own: which jobs change,
    what happens to privacy when software reads faces, who is responsible when an automated
    decision is wrong. Second, <strong>extended reality</strong> — virtual reality (a fully
    synthetic world, headset and motion tracking) and augmented reality (digital content
    overlaid on the real one, from phone apps to smart glasses). XR is a direct consumer of the
    shrink: it needs two high-resolution displays, a dozen sensors and graphics rendering at
    90+ frames per second, all battery-powered and light enough to wear — a spec sheet that
    was physically impossible in 2005. Both technologies also stress the older parts of the
    chapter: they demand operating systems that schedule real-time sensor data, storage
    hierarchies that stream gigabytes, and interfaces beyond the GUI. The syllabus's request
    that you discuss their impact on everyday life is really a question about the curve above
    continuing — and, as the flattening of its last decade hints, about what happens if it
    does not.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three refinements take you beyond the syllabus summary. First, the fetch–decode–execute
    loop as described is the honest skeleton, but real CPUs overlap it: <em>pipelining</em>
    keeps decode running while the next instruction is fetched and the previous one executes —
    like a laundry line where washer, dryer and folding table all work at once. Add
    <em>multi-core</em> (several complete CPUs on one chip) and the gigahertz number stops
    being the whole story: a 4-core 2 GHz chip outperforms a 1-core 3 GHz chip on almost
    everything modern, because software is built to split across cores. Second, memory
    technology continues to migrate: flash storage (the SSD's technology) began as a way to be
    "RAM that doesn't forget", and modern phones use their fast storage almost as working
    memory — the RAM/ROM/SSD boundaries the syllabus draws are real conceptually but physically
    converging. Third, the historical structure is worth seeing: RAM and ROM's division of
    labour (volatile workspace, fixed bootstrap) descends from the von Neumann architecture of
    1945 — program and data stored together in one memory — which is why the CPU's loop has
    looked structurally the same for eighty years even as its speed improved by factors of
    millions. Moore's law changed the numbers; the architecture is the constant, and that is
    why this chapter's vocabulary — bus, register, interrupt, kernel — will still describe
    machines built long after the current syllabus expires.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE ICT 0417 syllabus 2026–2028, section 1 "Types and components of computer systems" (1.1–1.5); Intel, processor specifications (4004, 386, Pentium, Pentium 4, Core i7); Apple, M1 and M1 Ultra technical specifications (2020–2022); G. Moore, <em>Cramming more components onto integrated circuits</em>, Electronics (1965); J. von Neumann, First Draft of a Report on the EDVAC (1945).</p>
